      "plainText": "with units of metres per second:\n{--Jason Harrop, 2026-09-01T12:43:00Z: $$\\mathrm{m}\\mathrm{\\backslash ,s\\^\\{-1\\}}.$$--}{++Jason Harrop, 2026-09-01T12:43:00Z: $$\\mathrm{m}\\mathrm{\u2009}{\\mathrm{s}}^{\\mathrm{-1}}\\mathrm{.}$$++}\nHeight above ground is\n$$z\\mathrm{.}$$\nAir density is\n$$\\rho$$\nin\n{--Jason Harrop, 2026-09-01T12:43:00Z: $$\\mathrm{kg}\\mathrm{\\backslash ,m\\^\\{-3\\}}.$$--}{++Jason Harrop, 2026-09-01T12:43:00Z: $$\\mathrm{kg}\\mathrm{\u2009}{\\mathrm{m}}^{\\mathrm{-3}}\\mathrm{.}$$++}\nPower is $P$, normally in MW, and energy is $E$, normally in MWh or GWh.\nWind direction will usually be denoted by\n$$\\theta\\mathrm{.}$$\nA bar denotes an average:\n{--Jason Harrop, 2026-09-01T12:43:00Z: $$\\bar{U}\\mathrm{.}$$--}{++Jason Harrop, 2026-09-01T12:43:00Z: $$U.$$++}\nAn expectation over a probability distribution is written\n{--Jason Harrop, 2026-09-01T12:43:00Z: $$E[U].$$--}{++Jason Harrop, 2026-09-01T12:43:00Z: $$E\\left[U\\right]\\mathrm{.}$$++}\nThese are related concepts but are not always interchangeable.\n0.5 Time averages matter\nA phrase such as \u201cwind speed is 8 m/s\u201d is incomplete without knowing whether this means:\ninstantaneous wind;\n1-second average;\n10-minute average;\nhourly average;\ndaily average;\nannual mean.\nBecause wind-to-power conversion is nonlinear,\n{--Jason Harrop, 2026-09-01T12:43:00Z: $$P(E[U])\\ne E[P(U)].$$--}{++Jason Harrop, 2026-09-01T12:43:00Z: $$P(E\\left[U\\right])\\mathrm{\u2260}E\\left[P(U)\\right]\\mathrm{.}$$++}\nTemporal resolution will therefore be treated as part of the definition of a wind quantity, not as incidental metadata.\n\n",
