      "plainText": "[core properties: creator: Jason Harrop; revision: 1; lastModifiedBy: Jason Harrop; created: 2026-09-03T01:19:00Z; modified: 2026-09-03T01:19:00Z]\n[application properties: Application: Microsoft Office Word; AppVersion: 16.0000; Template: Normal.dotm; TotalTime: 0; DocSecurity: 0]\nwith units of metres per second:\n$$\\mathrm{m}\\mathrm{\u2009}{\\mathrm{s}}^{\\mathrm{-1}}\\mathrm{.}$$\nHeight above ground is\n$$z\\mathrm{.}$$\nAir density is\n$$\\rho$$\nin\n$$\\mathrm{kg}\\mathrm{\u2009}{\\mathrm{m}}^{\\mathrm{-3}}\\mathrm{.}$$\nPower is $P$, normally in MW, and energy is $E$, normally in MWh or GWh.\nWind direction will usually be denoted by\n$$\\theta\\mathrm{.}$$\nA bar denotes an average:\n$$U.$$\nAn expectation over a probability distribution is written\n$$E\\left[U\\right]\\mathrm{.}$$\nThese are related concepts but are not always interchangeable.\n0.5 Time averages matter\nA phrase such as \u201cwind speed is 8 m/s\u201d is incomplete without knowing whether this means:\n\uf0b7\tinstantaneous wind;\n\uf0b7\t1-second average;\n\uf0b7\t10-minute average;\n\uf0b7\thourly average;\n\uf0b7\tdaily average;\n\uf0b7\tannual mean.\nBecause wind-to-power conversion is nonlinear,\n$$P(E\\left[U\\right])\\mathrm{\u2260}E\\left[P(U)\\right]\\mathrm{.}$$\nTemporal resolution will therefore be treated as part of the definition of a wind quantity, not as incidental metadata.\n\n",
